--TEST--
A constructor invoked reflectively faces the check the `new` at that call site faces, so a `private` one builds from inside the class and is refused from outside — `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::construct
// `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`: acting on a member runs
// through exactly the check ordinary code *at that call site* would face, and
// a constructor is a member like any other, reached through the same
// `Core\Reflect\ClassInfo` a property read is reached through. So the same
// `private` constructor is asked for from two sites and both answers are
// printed — a door that
// simply refused every `private` constructor would look right on the refusal
// alone.
class Ledger {
    public int $opening;

    private function constructor(int $opening) {
        $this->opening = $opening;
    }

    // `new Ledger(5)` compiles here, so the reflective construction written
    // here reaches the same constructor: this is the singleton door `private`
    // was written for, and reflection neither opens nor closes it.
    public static function open(int $opening): mixed {
        var $described = Core\Reflect::forClass("Ledger");
        if ($described == null) {
            return null;
        }
        return $described->construct([$opening]);
    }
}

// A `public` constructor, so the refusal below is a visibility answer rather
// than this door being shut to every class alike.
class Tally {
    public int $total;

    public function constructor(int $total) {
        $this->total = $total;
    }
}

var $tally = Core\Reflect::forClass("Tally");
var $ledger = Core\Reflect::forClass("Ledger");
if ($tally == null) {
    echo "unreachable", "\n";
} else if ($ledger == null) {
    echo "unreachable", "\n";
} else {
    // Built, and built with what it was given: the instance is read back
    // through the description that made it.
    mixed $counted = $tally->construct([7]);
    echo "public built: ", $tally->get($counted, "total"), "\n";

    // Out here `new Ledger(1)` is `E0471` at the checker. The reflective one
    // is refused too, at run time, because this door resolves its class by
    // name — and in the erased call's own words, since it is that check.
    try {
        mixed $sealed = $ledger->construct([1]);
        echo "outside built", "\n";
    } catch (LogicError $refused) {
        echo "outside refused: ", $refused->message, "\n";
    }

    // And from inside the class, where the ordinary `new` is allowed.
    mixed $opened = Ledger::open(5);
    echo "inside built: ", $ledger->get($opened, "opening"), "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
public built: 7
outside refused: `Ledger::constructor` is not public, and a `mixed` receiver is outside every class
inside built: 5
